# Noktiq

**Point. Note. Paste. Visual change requests for React and Vue.**

Select an element on your development website, describe the change and copy a request into your coding agent. Noktiq includes the source file, line and column when available, plus selectors and page context. No AI account or API key is required.

{{INSTALL_STATUS}}

## Why Noktiq exists

A small visual change can turn into a long explanation: which button, which card, which component? Repeating the description and searching for the wrong target can spend more time and tokens than the change itself.

Noktiq grew out of that frustration. Point at the element, write the change where you see it, and copy its context into the coding agent you already use. **Less time explaining where. More context for making the change.**

The aim is fewer clarification rounds, unnecessary code searches and avoidable tokens across a complete task. A structured request may be longer than a short prompt; any saving depends on the agent, model, project and request. We have not published a token-savings benchmark or percentage, and savings are not guaranteed.

## React + Vite

```tsx
import { Noktiq } from 'noktiq/react';

export default function App() {
  return <>{/* Your application */}<Noktiq enabled={import.meta.env.DEV} /></>;
}
```

```ts
// vite.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import react from '@vitejs/plugin-react';
import { noktiq } from 'noktiq/vite';

export default defineConfig({ plugins: [noktiq(), react()] });
```

## Vue + Vite

```vue
<script setup>
import { Noktiq } from 'noktiq/vue';
const isDev = import.meta.env.DEV;
</script>

<template>
  <!-- Your application -->
  <Noktiq :enabled="isDev" />
</template>
```

```ts
// vite.config.ts
import { defineConfig } from 'vite';
import vue from '@vitejs/plugin-vue';
import { noktiq } from 'noktiq/vite';

export default defineConfig({ plugins: [noktiq(), vue()] });
```

Start your application's development server (usually `npm run dev`) and open its URL. Restart it after editing plugin configuration. Installing the package alone does not display the mascot.

## Development and production

Keep the development condition in the examples; you do not need to delete the component before deploying.

| Environment | With the documented development condition |
| --- | --- |
| Development server | Nok appears for selection and notes. |
| Production build/local preview | Nok stays hidden, including on localhost. |
| Deployed production build | Visitors do not see Nok, its menu or pins. |

`enabled` defaults to `false`. Vite uses `import.meta.env.DEV`; Next.js uses `process.env.NODE_ENV === 'development'`; Nuxt uses `import.meta.dev`. Hardcoding `enabled` to true mounts the toolbar in production too. The `-D` install flag does not replace the runtime condition. The source plugin skips production builds; hiding the UI does not guarantee removal of all library JavaScript from a bundle.

## Workflow

1. Click Nok to enter selection mode.
2. Select an element, enter your request and save the note.
3. Copy one note or all notes into your existing coding agent.

Drag Nok to reposition it. Notes support text, multiple elements and area selection; parent navigation; source links; live target status; rebinding; computed styles; and configurable output detail. Notes stay in your browser and are synchronized between tabs on the same origin.

Standard copying prioritizes source locations, selectors, non-empty text and instance identifiers. Full framework component chains and computed styles appear only in Detailed. Secondary component-file hints and CSS class lists are omitted from Standard when the exact element source is known; fallback hints and uncertainty warnings remain when it is not. Compact also omits viewport and style details. Individual and combined copying use the same rules in React and Vue, including supported Next.js, Nuxt, Vite and Webpack integrations.

## Compatibility

Tested examples cover React 18/19, Vue 3.4/3.5, Next.js 14/15/16, Nuxt 3/4, Vite 6/7/8 and Webpack 5. These tests do not guarantee every version or component combination. Closed Shadow DOM, cross-origin iframes and individual canvas/3D objects are outside the supported selection scope.

For Next.js, Nuxt, Webpack, editor configuration and all options, see the packaged [developer guide (Turkish)](docs/guide.tr.md). The [captured request](docs/walkthrough-request.md) shows a real output example. Demo recordings and repository-only validation reports are intentionally excluded from the npm archive.

## Common questions

**No mascot?** Render the component once with a true development condition. A production preview keeps it hidden. The source plugin alone does not create the toolbar.

**No source line?** Configure the plugin and restart the development server. Unsupported source patterns fall back to known component/ancestor context and DOM identifiers. Captured lines identify rendering code; CSS and event handlers may live elsewhere.

**No clipboard permission?** Copy manually from the selectable output preview using Ctrl/Cmd+C.

**Where are notes stored?** In the browser, separated by page and origin; changing ports or domains changes the note group. Saved changes synchronize across tabs. Use **Notes** for individual edit/copy/delete actions and **Clear all** to remove the page's stored notes.

## Privacy and license

Noktiq does not send notes to an AI service. Inspect copied context before sharing it, and mark sensitive regions with `data-noktiq-private`. Browser storage failures are reported; memory-only notes are lost on a full reload.

